ious player discarded F3? Previous player history: ... Play J2 (last). No F3. Across player: Play F3 (in their history: ... Play J1, Play F3, ...). So F3 has been seen. J1: across player played J1 twice? Actually across: Play J1, later Play J1? Wait: \"Play J1, Play F3, Play B9, Play F4, Play J1.\" So two J1 discarded. Next player didn't discard J1. So J1 relatively safe too.  \n\nBut we need offensive shape. If I discard B tile, I lose potential chow. If I discard W4 or T6, I break a pair. Keeping pairs is good for potential pungs or seven pairs? But seven pairs needs seven pairs, not likely here.  \n\nMaybe aim for All Types + some pung patterns. Keep B tiles for possible chow, keep pairs for possible pung, discard one honor.  \n\nBetween F3 and J1: J1 is dragon, which gives Dragon Pung (2 pts) if I get pung. F3 is wind, gives only Pung of Honors (1 pt) unless it's seat/prevalent wind. Since my seat wind is F1, F3 gives only 1 pt if pung. So J1 slightly more valuable. Also, from unshown tiles, J1:2 left, J3:4 left, J2:1 left. So J1 still two left, possible to draw.  \n\nBut I have only one J1, need two more. Similarly F3: unshown F3:2 left. Both similar.  \n\nI think discard F3 to reduce honors and focus on B suit.  \n\nLet's evaluate hand after discarding F3:  \nHand: W4x2, T6x2, B2, B3, B4, B5, B6, J1.  \nMeld: F1 pung.  \n\nThen I need three melds + pair. Possible melds:  \n- B2 B3 B4 chow (need to draw B2 or B3 or B4 to complete? Actually I have one each, so need one more of any to complete? Wait, chow needs three tiles in sequence. I have one each of B2,B3,B4, that's already a chow set! But they are not yet melded; I can use them as a concealed chow if I keep them. So that's one potential chow.  \n- B4 B5 B6 chow (I have one each B4,B5,B6) \u2014 that's another potential chow.  \nBut they share B4. So I can only use one of these chows unless I get another B4.  \nAlternatively, could make B3 B4 B5 chow.  \n\nSo I have many B chow possibilities. Also pairs: W4 pair, T6 pair, could become pungs.  \n\nGoal: try to form hand like: chow (B234), chow (B456), pung (W4 or T6), pair (the other). That would be All Chows? No, includes pung.  \n\nBut with F1 pung already exposed, I have one pung. So remaining needed: two chows and a pair.  \n\nIf I get another B2, B3, B4, B5, or B6, I can complete a chow.  \n\nSo discarding F3 seems okay.  \n\nOther option: discard J1? Similar.  \n\nDiscard B tile? Would break chow potential.  \n\nDiscard W4 or T6? Would break pair, reducing pung chance.  \n\nSo among non-B, discard honor.  \n\nChoose F3 or J1? Possibly F3 because J1 is dragon for Dragon Pung pattern.  \n\nBut Dragon Pung needs pung; I only have one J1.  \n\nGiven I already have F1 pung for 4 pts, I might aim for All Types (6) + Dragon Pung (2) + maybe others. That would reach 12+. So keeping J1 might be good.  \n\nBut to get Dragon Pung, I need two more J1. Possible but not high probability.  \n\nAlternatively, could aim for Half Flush with B + honors: discard W4 and T6 eventually. But that's longer term.  \n\nGiven current hand, I think discarding F3 is fine.  \n\nReason: F3 is a wind not matching my seat/prevalent, only worth 1 pt if pung, less valuable than J1 (dragon, 2 pts). Keeping J1 preserves chance for Dragon Pung. Also, discarding F3 reduces honor clutter, keeping hand more flexible for B chows.  \n\nFinal choice: Play F3.  \n\nAnswer: Play F3.  \n\nSituation: I have a hand with a F1 pung (seat/prevalent wind, 4 points), several Dots tiles forming potential chows, and pairs of W4 and T6. The hand is mixed with honors and three suits.  \nEvaluation: Discarding F3 preserves the Dragon tile for possible Dragon Pung, maintains B suit chow potential, and removes a less valuable honor.  \nReason: F3 is not my wind and only yields 1 point if made into a pung, whereas J1 offers 2 points for Dragon Pung. Keeping B tiles and pairs offers more flexibility to form melds.  \nAnswer: Play F3"}
